==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪnˈglɔriəs/|/ɪnˈgloʊriəs/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|in|glo|ri|ous|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
{{prefix|in|glorious}}; from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|inglōrius|lang=la}}; first known use: 1565-75.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[ignominious|Ignominious]]; [[disgraceful]].
#* {{circa|1596}} [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''King John'', act 5, sc. 1,
#*: O '''inglorious''' league!
#*: Shall we, upon the footing of our land,
#*: Send fair-play orders and make compromise,
#*: Insinuation, parley and base truce
#*: To arms invasive?
#* '''1818''', [[w:Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley|Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley]], ''Frankenstein'', ch. 6,
#*: Resolved to pursue no '''inglorious''' career, he turned his eyes toward the East.
#* '''1906''', [[w:Jack London|Jack London]], ''White Fang'', ch. 4,
#*: He cast about in his mind for a way to beat a retreat not too '''inglorious'''.
# Not [[famous]]; [[obscure]].

